From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 01:03:56 +0000
Subject: clocksource: sun5i: allow enabling the high-speed timer on 64-bit
 sunxi

The high-speed timer driver binds "allwinner,sun20i-d1-hstimer", which
the H616 device tree already uses as the fallback for its
"allwinner,sun50i-h616-hstimer" node at 0x3005000. The symbol, however,
only has a prompt under COMPILE_TEST and is selected by the 32-bit
mach-sunxi Kconfig alone, so arm64 and RISC-V sunxi kernels cannot
enable it and the node never probes.

Make the prompt visible on ARCH_SUNXI. The driver registers its
clocksource and clockevent at rating 340, below the arch timer (400 and
450) and the sun4i timer (350), so enabling it adds a second hardware
time base without changing which one the kernel uses.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/clocksource/Kconfig | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig b/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/clocksource/Kconfig
@@ -161,12 +161,17 @@ config SUN4I_TIMER
 	  Enables support for the Sun4i timer.
 
 config SUN5I_HSTIMER
-	bool "Sun5i timer driver" if COMPILE_TEST
+	bool "Sun5i timer driver" if COMPILE_TEST || ARCH_SUNXI
 	select CLKSRC_MMIO
 	depends on COMMON_CLK
 	help
 	  Enables support the Sun5i timer.
 
+	  The 32-bit sunxi platforms select it. On 64-bit (ARM64, RISC-V)
+	  sunxi SoCs such as the H616 and D1 the block registers as an
+	  additional clocksource and clockevent device, rated below the
+	  architected timer.
+
 config TEGRA_TIMER
 	bool "Tegra timer driver" if COMPILE_TEST
 	select CLKSRC_MMIO
-- 
